74609375, -0.01849365234375, 0.0032482147216796875, 0.053558349609375, 0.00543212890625, 0.005344390869140625, 0.0212554931640625, 0.0206146240234375, 0.016510009765625, 0.0203094482421875, -0.0221710205078125, -0.02642822265625, 0.006526947021484375, 0.006130218505859375, 0.011016845703125, -0.0007157325744628906, -0.0002498626708984375, -0.00897979736328125, 0.01000213623046875, -0.00011026859283447266, 0.01280975341796875, 0.006649017333984375, -0.002086639404296875, -0.005359649658203125, -0.00354766845703125, -0.0106658935546875, -0.008209228515625, -0.00110626220703125, 0.020599365234375, 0.0205230712890625, 0.01904296875, -0.005008697509765625, 0.0074310302734375, 0.017242431640625, -0.0039825439453125, 0.06658935546875, -0.04449462890625, 0.018890380859375, 0.0162200927734375, -0.0355224609375, 0.0198211669921875, -0.01605224609375, 0.005603790283203125, 0.015716552734375, -0.03448486328125, 0.0013427734375, -0.00739288330078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies. In this realm, they face the challenge of dealing with increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and meshed topologies. This paper\u2019s main contributions are the development of new algorithmic techniques to globally optimize the operation of district heating networks with realistic nonlinearities and cycles, and the demonstration of their effectiveness on both synthetic and real-world benchmarks. The novelty lies in the integration of several methodological advances into a spatial branch-and-bound framework, enabling efficient global optimization for complex, time-coupled networks.\n\nWe consider a stationary, nonlinear network model that captures key physical aspects of district heating systems, including nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, and realistic operational constraints. Unlike simplified linear or tree-based models, our approach accommodates meshed topologies and multiple suppliers, reflecting the complexity of real-world networks.\n\nTo accelerate the solution performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for one time step, we introduce the following methodological innovations:\n1. Exclusion of cyclic flow: Prevents non-physical flow cycles, reducing the feasible space.\n2. Symmetry exploitation between supply and return networks: Identifies and eliminates redundant solutions arising from network symmetry.\n3. Reduction of temperature mixing constraints: Simplifies the model by aggregating mixing constraints where possible.\n4. Novel primal heuristics: Provides high-quality feasible solutions early in the optimization process.\n5. Improved branching rules: Enhances the efficiency of the branch-and-bound search.\n\nThe proposed methods are evaluated on a set of generated and real-world benchmark network instances. The generated instances include networks with up to 20 nodes, multiple cycles, and several suppliers, designed to test scalability and robustness. The real-world benchmarks are based on anonymized data from operational district heating systems, featuring up to 15 nodes and realistic demand profiles, ensuring practical relevance. On the generated benchmark instances, our methods increase the number of solved instances from 40% to over 90% and reduce median runtime from 600 seconds to under 250 seconds. For the real-world benchmark instances, the resulting algorithm produces solutions with guaranteed quality in reasonable run time (typically under 10 minutes per instance).\n\nFor multiple time steps that are coupled by a storage, a time decomposition approach is investigated. Under the assumption that storage operation can be decoupled across time steps when storage constraints are not binding, this approach is shown to yield an optimal solution. However, if storage constraints are tight or highly nonlinear, optimality may not be guaranteed. On a small example network with 6 nodes and 2 suppliers, this decomposition is able to compute optimal solutions in less than a second, while solving the complete time-coupled problem is not possible within one hour.\n\nFuture work includes extending the approach to larger networks, incorporating additional operational uncertainties, and exploring integration with electricity and gas networks."}}